{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"securing-s3-buckets","skill_description":"Create and secure S3 buckets following AWS best practices for access control, encryption, monitoring, and remediation of misconfigurations. Use when the user wants to secure a new bucket, audit an existing bucket, fix a security finding, configure encryption, or enable logging and monitoring. Do NOT use for general S3 data operations, S3 Tables setup, or discovering existing data assets.\n\n\nServices: s3, kms, cloudtrail, guardduty, config, accessanalyzer\nTasks: secure, audit, remediate, encrypt, monitor\nPersona: developer, security-engineer\nWorkload: storage, compliance\n*","skill_name":"securing-s3-buckets"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS HealthOmics now supports IAM session policies - AWS","context":"AWS HealthOmics, a HIPAA-eligible service, has introduced support for IAM session policies. This feature allows users to restrict permissions for individual runs without creating multiple IAM roles. Previously, users had to create separate IAM roles for each tenant or run to limit permissions. IAM session policies are inline policies that limit a run's permissions without modifying the underlying service role. The effective permissions during a run are the intersection of permissions from both the identity-based policy and the temporary session policy. This feature is particularly useful for multi-tenant applications, enabling users to grant access to specific Amazon S3 buckets or objects for a single run. IAM session policies can also be used to isolate access to sensitive resources on a per-run basis. The support for IAM session policies is available in all AWS Regions where AWS HealthOmics is available. Users can learn how to configure IAM session policies by visiting the AWS HealthOmics User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/omics-iam-session-policy/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS Textract docs on synchronous AnalyzeDocument S3 access permission model."}
